Negative control for the T3.11 handler-scope spec (`AC8.3`).

Arming answers this module's own endpoints and NOTHING else — the brand, its
settings, the app config and the session still reach staging while a preset is
on. The mutant leaves every declared route in place and hangs one catch-all off
the end, so the module's own reads and writes answer exactly as before and the
served bodies are unchanged. What it takes over is the rest of the app.

What must go red is SCOPE, not the answer. The resolver passes an unrecorded
request through, so a spec reading only the eventual response stays green while
the worker has in fact taken the whole app: forcing `empty` then leaves an
undefined brand over every forced page for the rest of the tab's life, because
the singletons that booted it never re-ask. Read the HANDLER LIST — require a
chrome url to match no handler, and require the list to be exactly the declared
routes with nothing beside them.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the handler-scope spec — matches no request outside the module, and
registers one handler per declared route and no catch-all beside them.

diff --git a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/handlers.ts b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/handlers.ts
--- a/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/handlers.ts
+++ b/playgrounds/labs-nuxt/modules/scenarios/runtime/force/handlers.ts
@@ -108,4 +108,7 @@ export function createForceHandlers(
   const resolve = presetResolver(preset, bodies, failure);
 
-  return map(moduleRoutes(feature, bodies), route => http.all(route, resolve));
+  return [
+    ...map(moduleRoutes(feature, bodies), route => http.all(route, resolve)),
+    http.all("*", resolve)
+  ];
 }
